---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<LegalPage accent="License" title="Template license" description={`One license for every ${site.name} template, in Framer or as code: free templates for any number of projects, paid ones for one project each, never for resale.`} updated="November 3, 2026">
  <p>
    This is the license for every {site.name} template (each one a "Template"): the Framer version and the code
    version, free or paid, wherever you got it. The Licensor is Vladimir Kotenev, trading as {site.name}. The same text
    is in the file LICENSE.md inside every code download.
  </p>
  <p>
    Framer templates are delivered through the Framer Marketplace. Framer's own marketplace terms also apply to how you
    get and remix them; this license sets what you may do with the design.
  </p>

  <h2>Free and paid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free templates</strong>: use them for as many websites as you like, for yourself or for clients.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paid templates</strong> (including the paid code version of a free template): one license covers
      <strong>one</strong> website (a "Project"), for yourself or for one client. Each extra Project needs its own
      license. The license starts when you have paid in full.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Apart from the number of Projects, the rules below are the same for every Template.</p>

  <h2>You can</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Edit the design, text, colors and images however you like.</li>
    <li>Use the finished Project for commercial purposes, including selling products or services on it.</li>
    <li>Hand the finished Project to your client. The client owns the Project, not the Template as a template.</li>
    <li>Keep backups of the files for your own Project.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>You cannot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Resell, sublicense, rent, lend, give away or share the Template or any part of it, changed or not, free or paid.</li>
    <li>Put the files or a remixable copy in a public repository, a file-sharing site or a public Framer remix link. A repository that holds only your finished Project is fine.</li>
    <li>Turn the Template, or a version of it, into a template, theme, starter, boilerplate, kit, UI library or website builder, or into any product that lets others reuse it as a base.</li>
    <li>Present the Template as your own design to sell as a template.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ownership</h2>
  <p>
    The Template stays the property of the Licensor. This license is non-exclusive, worldwide and perpetual, and gives
    you the right to use the Template as described above and nothing more.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party parts</h2>
  <p>
    Templates use fonts and icons with their own licenses, such as Inter (SIL Open Font License 1.1) and Lucide icons
    (ISC License). They keep those licenses. The README of each code version lists its credits.
  </p>

  <h2>Images</h2>
  <p>
    Demo images, screenshots and photos in a Template show the layout. Replace them with your own before you publish,
    unless the README says an image is free to use.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    We may publish new versions. If we do, Framer versions update on the Framer Marketplace and code versions on your
    purchase page on Contra. Updates are a courtesy, not a promise.
  </p>

  <h2>Payment and refunds</h2>
  <p>
    Paid Framer templates are paid for on Framer, under Framer's terms. Code versions are digital products and all sales
    are final. See the <a href="/refunds/">refund policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    The Template is provided as is, without warranty of any kind. To the extent the law allows, the Licensor is not
    liable for any damage that comes from using it.
  </p>

  <h2>Ending the license</h2>
  <p>If you break these terms, the license ends and you must stop using the Template and delete the files.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about the license: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.</p>
</LegalPage>
